INT. PLANETARIUM - DOME THEATRE - NIGHT

A hushed circular hall. Rows of reclining seats fan out toward a central projector like a black beetle. Overhead, a star field turns slowly on the white dome, thousands of points of light so soft they seem to breathe. The air smells of carpet and warm dust.

Rows of empty seats. Not quite empty.

At the lectern at the front stands LAVINIA MORROW-ESPOSITO (30s, astronomer, wool cardigan, a laser pointer lit in her hand). She is rehearsing. She has done this speech a hundred times and has never said it right.

In the back row, half in shadow, sits ELIAN TREMAINE (30s, night guard, flashlight dark in his lap). He came in to check the doors and stayed.

LAVINIA
(to the empty seats)
Good evening, everyone. Welcome to the Southern Sky.

She sweeps the laser over the dome. A red dot dances among the stars.

LAVINIA (CONT'D)
(to the empty seats)
If you look up now, you'll see the constellation we call the Southern Cross.

She stops. Lowers the pointer. Mutters.

LAVINIA (CONT'D)
(muttering)
Too slow. They'll be asleep by the third star.

ELIAN
(from the dark)
I wasn't.

She jumps. The laser skitters across the dome. He half stands, flashlight raised like a white flag.

ELIAN (CONT'D)
Sorry. I didn't mean to scare you.

LAVINIA
How long have you been there?

ELIAN
Since Orion.

LAVINIA
That's the second slide.

ELIAN
Is it?

LAVINIA
(drily)
Then you've missed the introduction.

ELIAN
I liked what I heard.

She studies him: the grey uniform, the badge, the way he sits with his knees turned out, careful of the seat in front. He does not look like a man who has ever been in the way.

LAVINIA
I'm supposed to give this to a hundred schoolchildren tomorrow.

ELIAN
You'd be good at that.

LAVINIA
You haven't heard me.

ELIAN
I've heard enough.

She almost smiles. Taps the lectern with the pointer. The stars drift.

LAVINIA
Would you stay?

ELIAN
For the rehearsal?

LAVINIA
For the audience.

He sits back. Lays the flashlight on the carpet.

ELIAN
Go ahead.

She faces the dome. Takes a long breath. Her voice, when it comes, is lower, slower, as though she were speaking to a single person.

LAVINIA
(to the dome)
Good evening. Tonight we begin with a very old idea. That what you see up there is not above you but behind you.

ELIAN
(softly)
Behind?

LAVINIA
(to Elian)
The light you're looking at left its star before you were born. Some of it before anyone was born.

ELIAN
So it's gone.

LAVINIA
The star may be gone. The light isn't.

A silence. The dome turns. A faint wash of blue drifts across their faces.

LAVINIA (CONT'D)
(to herself, barely)
Say it. Just say it.

ELIAN
What?

LAVINIA
Nothing.

ELIAN
You were talking to yourself.

LAVINIA
It's a bad habit.

ELIAN
It's a nice one.

She steps out from behind the lectern. Walks up the aisle between the seats, the laser dark in her hand, until she is two rows from him. The dome throws pale constellations over her cardigan.

LAVINIA
Do you know what I do, in the daytime?

ELIAN
I'm guessing you look at things.

LAVINIA
I look at light that's already too late.

ELIAN
That sounds lonely.

LAVINIA
It's the best part of the job.

He smiles, a little crooked. She sits down in the row in front of his. Not beside. Not far.

LAVINIA (CONT'D)
(to the dome)
Good evening, and welcome.

Elian reclines. Folds his hands over his stomach. Looks up at the stars, and she looks up with him, and the two of them watch the sky wheel in the dark without saying anything.

FADE OUT.
